---
import InfoPage from "../layouts/InfoPage.astro";
import CtaBlock from "../components/CtaBlock.astro";
import FaqBlock from "../components/FaqBlock.astro";
import site from "../data/site.ts";

const title = "Is AI Safe on Your Salesforce Org?";
const description =
  "The two real risks of connecting AI to Salesforce are write access and your data leaving. How to judge any tool on both, and how sf-intelligence handles them.";
const dateModified = "2026-10-10";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Is it safe to connect an AI assistant to my Salesforce org?",
    a: "It depends on what the connection can do. Ask two questions of any tool: can it change the org, and where does the org’s data go? sf-intelligence can’t change anything, and it keeps the org’s metadata on your computer.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does my data go to the AI provider?",
    a: "Your AI assistant sees the questions you ask and the answers it gets back, as it would with anything you paste into it. sf-intelligence itself uploads nothing: the snapshot stays in a folder on your computer. Use an assistant your company has approved.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can someone trick it with prompt injection?",
    a: "It refuses requests to change things, attempts to override its instructions, and requests to pull out sensitive values, before any tool runs. And because it has no write code and its snapshot holds no records, there’s little a tricked assistant could reach through it.",
  },
  {
    q: "We’re planning Agentforce. Does this help?",
    a: "It doesn’t run inside Agentforce or replace it. It gives you an accurate, read-only map first: sensitive-looking fields, what existing agents can reach, broad access and debt. See the <a href=\"/use-cases/agent-readiness-audit\">agent-readiness audit</a>.",
  },
];
const toText = (html: string): string => html.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, "").replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim();

const jsonld = [
  {
    "@type": "TechArticle",
    "@id": `${site.url}/ai-safety#article`,
    headline: "Is it safe to connect AI to your Salesforce org?",
    description,
    dateModified,
    inLanguage: "en",
    author: { "@id": `${site.url}/#author` },
    publisher: { "@id": `${site.url}/#org` },
    mainEntityOfPage: { "@id": `${site.url}/ai-safety#webpage` },
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ "@type": "Question", name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: toText(f.a) } })),
  },
];
---

<InfoPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/ai-safety"
  heading="Is AI safe on your Salesforce org?"
  lede="Pointing an AI assistant at a production org raises two fair worries: could it change something, and could your data leave? Here’s how to judge any tool on both."
  crumbs={[{ name: "AI safety", path: "/ai-safety" }]}
  jsonld={jsonld}
  dateModified={dateModified}
>
  <div class="answer-first narrow">
    <span class="label">Short answer</span>
    <p>The risk comes from two things: <strong>write access</strong> and <strong>data leaving your control</strong>. A tool that can’t write and keeps the org’s data on your computer removes most of it. sf-intelligence is built that way: it has no write code, answers from a local snapshot, and refuses requests to change things or pull out sensitive values.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="risks">
    <h2 id="risks">The two real risks</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>It changes something</h3>
        <p>An assistant that can write can change field values, turn off automation, delete records or run code, because it misunderstood you or because someone manipulated it. Any tool that can deploy or edit carries this risk.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>Your data leaves</h3>
        <p>A tool that sends your org’s metadata or records to its own servers has moved them outside your security boundary. That can be fine, but it should be a decision you make, not a default.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="how">
    <h2 id="how">How sf-intelligence handles each one</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Worry</th><th scope="col">What happens</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Changing the org</th><td>It can’t. There is no create, update, delete or deploy code. Only commands you run, like <code>refresh</code>, contact Salesforce, and they only read.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Data leaving your computer</th><td>The snapshot stays in a folder on your computer and answers come from there. No telemetry by default.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Being tricked</th><td>Requests to change things, to ignore instructions, or to dump sensitive values are refused before any tool runs.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Customer records</th><td>The snapshot holds setup, not records. Record facts need the optional live mode, which you turn on per org and which only reads small, capped amounts.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">What’s in the package</th><td>The npm package is code only, and every release is checked for real org names before it ships.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Trusting an answer</th><td>Each answer names its sources, how sure it is, and what it couldn’t check.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="compare">
    <h2 id="compare">Local and read-only versus hosted connectors</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"><span class="sr-only">Topic</span></th><th scope="col">sf-intelligence</th><th scope="col">Typical hosted AI connector</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Where the org’s data is kept</th><td>Your computer</td><td>The vendor’s service</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Can write to the org</th><td>No</td><td>Often, where enabled</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Works offline</th><td>Yes, after one refresh</td><td>No</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Code you can read</th><td>Yes</td><td>Usually not</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Cost</th><td>Free</td><td>Often per seat or per org</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="meta-line">Hosted tools have real strengths too: no setup per laptop, shared dashboards, support contracts. <a href="/compare">Tool-by-tool comparisons</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block narrow" aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">Where it’s honest about limits</h2>
    <ul class="ticks minus">
      <li>Your AI assistant still sees the answers it asks for. Choose the assistant with that in mind.</li>
      <li>Answers are as fresh as your last refresh, and it says when the snapshot is stale.</li>
      <li>Code that builds queries at run time can’t be followed. “No references found” means no evidence, not a guarantee.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="faq">
    <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>
    <FaqBlock faqs={faqs} schema={false} />
  </section>

  <nav class="block narrow" aria-labelledby="more">
    <h2 id="more">Related</h2>
    <ul class="link-list">
      <li><a href="/trust">Safe to point at production</a><span> · the full trust model</span></li>
      <li><a href="/use-cases/agent-readiness-audit">Is the org ready for AI agents?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/use-cases/sharing-troubleshooting">Sharing and permission troubleshooting</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <CtaBlock />
</InfoPage>
